{
  "description": "issue #782: the `who`/position AGREEMENT INVARIANT itself, stated with no hard-coded coordinate. `spec/turtles-and-sprites.md:26` defines `who` as \"the turtle currently running turtle commands\" and `:105` evaluates the movement reporters \"for the current turtle\" — ONE current turtle — so any pair `who` and `ycor` can produce must be a state some single turtle really has. After a callee's `tell :b`, `ask who [ :seen = ycor ]` records the y of the very turtle `who` reports, and `print :seen == ycor` compares it with the y the caller's own `ycor` reports; `true` is possible only when both name the same turtle. `ask` restores the addressed set on exit (`:58`) — visible here as the second `ask` addressing `primitive` — so the trailing `ycor` is read under the same set the `ask` was entered with, and the comparison is not an artifact of the `ask`. The defect made this print `false`: `who` followed the shared pointer to :b while `ycor` read the caller's stale cached :a.",
